<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gormaz Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Castle of Gormaz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gormaz Castle is a fortress on a ridge above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a> river in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, one of the largest castles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and rebuilt in the 960s under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a>, it guarded the frontier against the Christian kingdoms of the north. Its walls stretch for about a kilometre, and it passed into Castilian hands in the eleventh century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gormaz-castle.jpg" alt="Gormaz Castle" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan F. Esteban Barahona · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gormaz,_Fortaleza_califal_p%C3%B1a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The castle stands 13 kilometres south-east of El Burgo de Osma, on a hill rising 100 metres above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a>, in a district dotted with watch-towers. In the 10th century the area was contested between Umayyad al-Andalus and the Christian kingdoms of the north, and an earlier fortification already stood here: Christian chronicles say Muslim forces retook it in 925 and 940. The present castle was built or rebuilt in 965–66 by Ghalib ibn Abd al-Rahman, a general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hakam-ii">al-Hakam II</a>, as a foundation stone bearing the caliph's name and the date confirms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 974 and 975 the fortress withstood a siege by Sancho of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, Garcia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Ramiro III of Leon until Ghalib relieved it in the summer of 975. Christian chronicles report that the Muslims recaptured it in 983. Fernando I is said to have taken it in 1059, but the occupation was perhaps brief, and by the end of the century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a> region was under the definitive control of Castile. Alfonso VI gave the castle to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> in 1087. It changed hands several times in the 14th-century war between Pedro I of Castile and Pedro IV of Aragon, and was later abandoned. Excavations began in 1922.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Built of masonry stone, the castle follows the ridge for about 380 metres, between 17 and 63 metres wide, behind walls about 10 metres high and set with 27 towers. Its main gate, to the south-west, has two horseshoe arches 1.26 metres apart, from which defenders could strike at attackers; the outer arch was framed by an alfiz with stucco decoration in the style of the Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madinat-al-zahra">Madinat al-Zahra</a>. A more heavily fortified citadel at the eastern end probably served as a keep, while the larger western part could house an army. Both parts held a cistern.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Castle of Gormaz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
